[=#oraclelimitations]
= Limitations =

The implementation of the Oracle protocol isn't 100% complete.  Some notable limitations follow:

* Oracle native network encryption and crypto-checksumming are not supported.  A client with SQLNET.ENCRYPTION_CLIENT or SQLNET.CRYPTO_CHECKSUM_CLIENT set to REQUIRED fails to connect with ORA-12660.  Any other setting works.
* Clients always log in with Oracle's challenge/response password exchange.  Cleartext password login is not supported.
* TLS/SSL (TCPS) and Kerberos are not supported between the client and SQL Relay.  Setting //tls// or //krb// on an Oracle listener has no effect, other than possibly a warning at startup.
* LOB locators are read-only.  LOBs can be bound into inserts and updates, and fetched LOBs can be read, but writing, appending, trimming or erasing through a locator, creating or freeing a temporary LOB, and asking whether a locator is open all fail with ORA-03001.  The session remains usable afterwards.
* A row's LOBs must be read before the next row is fetched.  Fetching the next row, re-executing, or closing the cursor invalidates the previous row's LOBs, and reading one afterwards, or binding it to a statement, fails with ORA-22275.  This only applies to an Oracle back-end.  Other back-ends, and OCI7 clients, get LOB values inline.
* Bound TIMESTAMP values (including those with a time zone) keep their fractional seconds only to the microsecond.  Digits beyond that are dropped.
* A bound TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE value with a named time zone region (eg. US/Eastern) rather than an offset is bound with the region's name when the client sends it in local time, but loses its region when the client sends it in UTC, and is bound as the same instant at +00:00.  Which one depends on the client's and the module's time zone file versions.  In local time, which reading of an hour that repeats when the clocks go back is lost, and a region the module doesn't know (one added to the time zone file after version 11) is refused with ORA-20007.
* A bound TIMESTAMP WITH LOCAL TIME ZONE value is passed to the back-end as the same instant at +00:00.  Into a TIMESTAMP or DATE column, or into text, it gives its UTC wall time, where a real Oracle server gives the wall time in the session's time zone.  The module ignores the time zone a client sets for its session.
* The protocol doesn't say which way a bind in a PL/SQL block travels, so every bind in a block is treated as in-out.  An OCI bind with no indicator variable that the block sets to NULL ends in ORA-01405, as it does on a real Oracle server, but only if the value went in non-NULL.  One that went in NULL and came back NULL is taken for a bind the block only reads, and the call succeeds.
* CLOB characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane (eg. most emoji) are garbled when read, and can cause text after them in the same read to be skipped.
* Cancelling a running statement (eg. with Ctrl-C in sqlplus) doesn't interrupt it.  The statement runs to completion first.
* Distributed and two-phase-commit/XA transactions, database startup and shutdown, and end-to-end tracing attributes (module, action and client identifier) are not supported, and fail with ORA-03001.  Setting a tracing attribute also causes the client's next call to fail.
* A fetch of more than 100,000 rows at once fails with ORA-20004, and a batch larger than 32MB fails with ORA-20005.
* OCI7 clients can only bind NUMBER, VARNUM, CHAR, VARCHAR, LONG and DATE values.
* A client that keeps many statements open at once, failed or not (eg. one with a client-side statement cache), needs the instance's //maxcursors// (default 5) set higher than that number, or new statements fail with ORA-01000.
----

